    "id" : 73,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 65.667, 510.667, 528.333, 532.0 ],
    "font" : "FqmbrjMyriadPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 7.98,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Dilutions series used for the determination of the PCR amplification efficiencies for each of the four MHS muscle tissues: 1; 1:5 dilution steps, 2; 1:3 dilution steps, 3; 1:3 dilution steps, 4; 1:3 dilution steps."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 74,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 55.0, 467.667, 292.0, 492.282 ],
    "font" : "YkftbwWarnockPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 9.78,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "tions of 1:3 dilution steps instead 4 or 5 dilutions of 1:5 dilution steps."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 76,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 55.333, 446.333, 227.0, 460.333 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "GdydvmMyriadPro-Semibold",
    "font size" : 9.18,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Determining the relative allele frequencies"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 77,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.333, 315.0, 292.333, 448.333 ],
    "font" : "YkftbwWarnockPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 9.78,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "After assessing the linearity of the reverse transcription reactions and determining the PCR amplification efficiencies, the samples were screened to determine the relative quantities of the two RYR 1 alleles in each of the four samples. To confirm accuracy and reproducibility, the allele frequencies were determined by performing three independent experiments. Targets were amplified in triplicate within each experiment. Intraand inter assay variabilities as obtained by screening sample #1 are shown in Table 5 and 6, respectively. As the results show, both intraand inter assay variations are low."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 79,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 54.667, 217.333, 292.667, 314.667 ],
    "font" : "YkftbwWarnockPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 9.78,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Several mathematical models are available for relative quantification during real-time PCR. There are some small differences between the individual models. Nevertheless, all relative quantification analyses are based on the assumption that the concentration of the template (e.g. cDNA) at a sample's crossing point is the same for every sample containing the same target cDNA. The model used in this study, is a rearranged version of the"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 75,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 302.333, 371.0, 541.333, 493.0 ],
    "font" : "YkftbwWarnockPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 9.78,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "efficiency calibrated mathematical method (see Equation 2) [28]. In this model, calculations are based on E and the Ct values of an unknown \"sample\" versus a \"calibrator\". The \"target\" is the nucleic acid of interest, while the \"reference\" is a nucleic acid that is found at constant copy number in all samples and serves as an endogenous control. The \"calibrator\" is typically a sample with a stable ratio of target-to-reference and can be used to normalize all samples within a run, but in addition provides a constant calibration point between several runs."
  }, {
    "type" : "formula",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Formula",
    "id" : 78,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 306.667, 313.333, 538.0, 358.333 ],
    "content" : "Ct sample Ct calibrator E ref) E ref) Normalized ratio calibrator E target ) Ct sample E target ) Ct (2)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 80,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 302.333, 213.667, 541.0, 310.333 ],
    "font" : "YkftbwWarnockPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 9.78,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In this study the relative expression levels of all targets were measured simultaneously in one target tissue. When the two RYR 1 alleles have identical mRNA expression levels it can be assumed that their HPRT:AS1 and HPRT:AS2 ratios will also be identical. Therefore, the two can be compared as long as the amplification efficiencies are corrected for. This eliminates the need for a calibrator, and if preferred also the reference. Here, the reference"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 81,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 55.667, 185.667, 361.0, 199.0 ],
    "font" : "LrjsdvMyriadPro-Bold",
    "font size" : 9.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 5: Intra-assay variability of real-time AS-PCR when screening #1 (n = 3)"
